#
# prima-materia — Theravada, the conditions under which the absorption does not arise
#
# A second file of one transmission and not a second transmission. pmt:Theravada
# and pmw:Visuddhimagga are declared in traditions/theravada.ttl and are only
# named here; no identifier is typed twice. The split is a repo convenience of
# the kind SPEC §10 allows — the file is a unit of the repository and not of the
# subject matter — and it was made because that file already carries six hundred
# lines and the twelfth chapter, and a diff over two chapters of a different
# question is easier to judge on its own.
#
# Chapters III and IV of the handbook, read at the wording on 2026-09-29, with
# four passages from VI, VIII, XII and XIII that belong to the same question
#
# Why these two chapters and not another corpus. The examinability-driven
# entrance of SPEC §14 asks for the transmissions that name terms, cautions and
# conditions of failure, and the twelfth chapter of this handbook was opened for
# that reason on 2026-09-24. What that run left standing is the layer the
# twelfth chapter presupposes: the two chapters in which the handbook says under
# which named conditions the absorption does not arise at all. They are the
# densest statement of failure conditions in the holding, and none of it was
# entered.
#
# Edition. Read in the English of Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II,
# Pali Text Society Translation Series, pages 97 to 504, which is public domain
# and reachable by the regular route, and in the English of Ñāṇamoli, The Path
# of Purification, for the section numbers; every passage below was read in
# both. The locator given first in each source is the page of the Pali Text
# Society Pali edition, which both translations print in brackets in the running
# text and which is therefore the one number a reader can find in either. The
# section numbers are Ñāṇamoli's, as elsewhere in this transmission's files,
# because Pe Maung Tin carries none.
#
# A citation finding about the earlier work on this handbook, recorded rather
# than silently corrected. The Chapter XII sources entered on 2026-09-24 give
# the Path of Purity Part II as PTS 1931. The copy read for this run carries no
# printing year on its title page at all, and 1931 is the year of Part III,
# which is Translation Series 21. The page ranges in those citations are correct
# for Part II. The year is therefore not verifiable from the copy read and is
# left out of the citations written below; whether to strike it from the earlier
# ones is a change to merged data and is filed, not done here.
#

#
# The ten impediments, which are the first thing the handbook asks for
#

pmc:DasaPalibodha a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The ten impediments to be severed before the training begins"@en, "Die zehn Hemmnisse, die vor dem Beginn der Übung abzuschneiden sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "Ten things the handbook names in a mnemonic verse as impediments to be cut off by one about to develop concentration: a dwelling, a family or body of supporters, the gains that are the four requisites, a class of pupils, building work, travel, kin, illness, the books, and the supernormal powers. The list is closed and the handbook treats each in turn."@en ;
    skos:note "The rule the handbook states immediately after the list is what makes it a finding rather than a catalogue: this is not an impediment to everyone. A dwelling impedes only the man who falls into anxiety over its repairs, or who has accumulated belongings in it, or whose mind is caught by some business connected with it; for another it is no impediment. The same qualification is made for the family, the gains, the books and the kin, each with its own criterion and most with a story attached. So nine of the ten are stated as relations between a man and a thing and not as properties of the thing, and a reader cannot establish from outside whether any of them obtains."@en ;
    skos:note "The tenth is the exception and is entered separately at pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha, because it is stated as a property of the powers themselves and because the handbook devotes two later chapters to what it here tells the aspirant to cut off."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.29-30, Pali PTS [90] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 105-106; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmp:PalibodhaChedana a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The severing of the impediments, with a measure attached to four of them"@en, "Das Abschneiden der Hemmnisse, mit einem Maß an vier von ihnen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation the handbook prescribes against each impediment, and it is graded rather than uniform. Against the gains: leave the company and wander alone where one is not known. Against the class of pupils: if they have learnt much, finish with them; if little, hand them to another teacher living not further off than a league; and only if that fails, take leave of them. Against building work: if little remains, finish it; if much and it belongs to the Order, hand it over to the Order; and if there is nobody to hand it to, leave it and go. Against illness: take medicine, and if after some days it has not been relieved, address the body in the terms the handbook gives and go on with the training."@en ;
    skos:note "Three of the four remedies are stated with a measure — a distance of one league, a quantity of work remaining, a number of days — and the fourth with an addressee. That is unusual in this holding and is the reason the practice is entered separately from the list: an instruction with a measure in it can be followed wrongly, and one without cannot."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.43-50, Pali PTS [93]-[95] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 108-111; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:SamadhiPresupposesPalibodhaChedana a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The training presupposes that the impediments have been severed"@en, "Die Übung setzt voraus, dass die Hemmnisse abgeschnitten sind"@de ;
    pm:dependentStep pmc:Samadhi ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:PalibodhaChedana ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:statedNecessity ;
    pm:consequenceOfSkipping "For two of the ten the handbook states the cost in the operative passage. Of the gains: from sunrise to the first watch of the night there is no interval in the coming and going, and he gets no opportunity to do the duties of an ascetic. Of building work: he falls into continual anxiety over what has been done and what has not been done. The first is a loss of the time the training needs; the second is the state of mind the training is to replace."@en ;
    skos:note "The strength is set at stated necessity on the two items that carry a consequence, and it is worth saying that the other eight do not. For those the handbook prescribes the severing and says nothing about what follows from omitting it, which under SPEC §3.5 would be a prescribed order and not a necessity. They are not entered as eight further nodes at a weaker strength, because the handbook states them as one list with one instruction; what is entered instead is the list at pmc:DasaPalibodha and the qualification that most of them are relations and not properties."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.43, Pali PTS [93] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 108; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)", "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.45, Pali PTS [94] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 109-110; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:AbadhaPalibodhaKala a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "Illness as an impediment, with a term after which it stops being one"@en, "Krankheit als Hemmnis, mit einer Frist, nach der sie keines mehr ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Illness is an impediment while it is being treated. The handbook sets a term on the treatment: if the ailment has not been relieved although he has taken medicine for some days, he is to blame himself, tell his body that he is not its slave and that it has been carried through beginningless time, and go on with the training without waiting for recovery."@en ;
    skos:note "The term is in the handbook and the length of it is not: some days is what it says. So the passage names the moment at which the practitioner is to stop treating an obstacle as an obstacle and does not say when that moment is, which leaves the decision with him and leaves nothing for a second person to check."@en ;
    skos:note "It is entered because it is the only place in these two chapters where the handbook sets a term against its own condition rather than against the practice. Everywhere else in them the term, where there is one, is a probation of the practice: three days in a lodging, three days on a posture."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.50, Pali PTS [95] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 111; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:GanthaPalibodhaVassani a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The books as an impediment, and the three elders who laid them down for a counted number of years"@en, "Die Bücher als Hemmnis, und die drei Ältesten, die sie eine gezählte Zahl von Jahren niederlegten"@de ;
    skos:definition "Learning is an impediment only for the man constantly occupied with it, and the handbook illustrates the release with three cases in which a number of years is given: an elder who left off recitation for nineteen years and reached sanctity in the twentieth, an elder of Karuliyagiri who did so for eighteen, and an elder who did so for thirty."@en ;
    skos:note "The numbers are the reason this is entered. A transmission that gives a length of time before an attainment has said something a life can be measured against, and this holding carries very little of the kind. What the three cases do not supply is the other side: no case is given of an elder who laid the books down for a counted number of years and did not reach it, and no number is named at which he should conclude anything."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.51-55, Pali PTS [96]-[97] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, pp. 111-113; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .

pmc:IddhiPannayaPalibodha a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:Visuddhimagga ;
    rdfs:label "The powers are an impediment for insight and are broken by the slightest thing"@en, "Die Kräfte sind ein Hemmnis für die Einsicht und werden vom Geringsten gebrochen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The tenth impediment. The supernormal powers are those of an average man; they are hard to manage, like a child lying on its back or like tender corn, and the slightest thing breaks them. They are an impediment for insight and not for concentration, since they are obtained when concentration is obtained. Therefore one who seeks insight is to cut off the impediment of the powers, and another man only the remaining nine."@en ;
    skos:note "This is a self-warning of an unusual kind: the handbook tells the aspirant, in its third chapter, to cut off the thing to which it devotes its twelfth and thirteenth. It does not withdraw the instruction later and it does not reconcile the two places. The ground it gives here is also the ground on which the eleventh chapter counts the powers among the five benefits of developing concentration — that they are obtained through concentration — so the same fact is made the reason for pursuing them and the reason for cutting them off."@en ;
    skos:note "What it warns against is not the exercise of the powers but the keeping of them: they are said to break at the slightest thing, and the two similes are of things that cannot be set down and left. The display of the powers is refused elsewhere and on another ground, and that stands at pmc:DisplayOfPowersRefused; the two cautions do not overlap."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:Iddhividha ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Theravada ;
    dcterms:source "Buddhaghosa, Visuddhimagga III.56, Pali PTS [97] (trans. Pe Maung Tin, The Path of Purity, Part II, Pali Text Society, p. 113; also trans. Ñāṇamoli 1956)" .
